-  "notes": "# LiquidTouch v1.9.5\n\n3 November 2026\n\n## Revision: search, keyboard and card holds\n\n3 November 2026 · build `2026-11-03-r1` · version remains **1.9.5**.\n\n- Full and Quick Search try available client query definitions, accept wrapped,\n  nested and usable partial responses, and recover through Spotify’s rendered\n  search view when direct searches fail. Quick Search restores its background;\n  stale native completions cannot replace the visible LiquidTouch page.\n- Holding on-screen Backspace erases immediately, pauses for 380 ms, then repeats\n  every 65 ms. Release, cancellation, moving off the key, focus loss, hiding the\n  keyboard and closing the interface stop it. Normal, Quick and history searches\n  use the same behavior. The release click does not erase an extra character.\n- Holding a card opens its menu without native artwork dragging, image ghosts,\n  text selection or the browser image callout. Scrolling and card swipes remain\n  available.\n\n**Already running 1.9.5:** download the revised full ZIP and rerun the normal\ninstaller. The existing updater rejects updates with the same version number;\nCheck now and JSON import cannot replace the original 1.9.5. Settings/history\nretain their existing storage keys. Older compatible versions may use the\nupdated 1.9.5 JSON normally.\n\nValidation: 12 production-controller regression suites passed with a simulated\nhost, plus syntax and package-integrity checks. Browser suites and live Spotify\nwere unavailable for this revision. The previous release’s 69 browser-suite\nresults below are historical, not a new result for this build.\n\n## Player and history\n\n- With lyrics off, the artwork and control column now share a vertical center. The song title and artist are centered within the control column. Larger artwork still flows below the header and can push content down; player scrollbars stay hidden.\n- Listening history uses LiquidTouch’s on-screen keyboard when its search field is touched and the local keyboard setting is enabled. Typing filters the journal; Enter hides the keyboard and keeps history open. The system keyboard remains available when the local keyboard is disabled.\n\n## Touch navigation\n\n- Horizontal tab and edge-Back gestures reveal the incoming LiquidTouch page while dragging, with the navigation selection following progress. Releasing beyond the configured threshold commits the change; short or cancelled gestures return to the current page.\n- Back inside nested action menus previews the previous menu. Dismissing history/action sheets exposes the page underneath as the sheet moves. Quick Search previews its incoming field before committing. Existing player, track-artwork and Settings gestures retain their independent thresholds and speeds.\n- Incoming pages are inert previews: they cannot intercept taps, start playback or share duplicate element IDs. They use bounded card/track previews and cached metadata; navigation commits without waiting for an extra network fetch.\n- Hold menus now animate from the selected card or artwork and anchor beside it on wider displays. Narrow displays use a bottom-docked menu. Holding, moving or cancelling does not also play/open the item. Existing Surface/menu speed and Reduce Motion settings apply.\n\n## Song Radio and search\n\n- **Go to song radio** resolves Spotify’s generated Song Radio playlist, opens it in LiquidTouch, places the selected song first and starts that song in the playlist’s playback context. It no longer starts a generic track station. Unavailable radio playlists show an inline error.\n- Full and Quick Search keep their own route while Spotify finishes startup. A stale host Home event cannot replace the first results page.\n- Failed startup searches get one bounded retry, re-reading client search bindings before falling back to the authenticated catalog service. Valid empty results are not retried. Persistent failures keep an explicit Retry action.\n\n## Optional Low-RAM mode\n\n- Settings → **Appearance & glass → Low-RAM mode** keeps fewer cached pages, images, searches, lyrics and details, reduces artwork concurrency, avoids adjacent-artwork preloading and releases blurred image backgrounds.\n- The interface retains touch navigation and artwork but simplifies blur effects. Some pages may reload. This is an optional reduction of LiquidTouch’s own retained resources, not a fixed RAM saving for Spotify.\n- Listening history, limits, lyric timing, settings and update-feed preferences remain saved. Turning the mode off restores the regular effects and caching policy.\n\n## Gallery and documentation\n\n- The repository README explains the theme/extension’s purpose, touchscreen features, accessibility, installation and updates, with a dedicated screenshot gallery.\n- A separate **LiquidTouch-Gallery.html** demonstrates the production UI with randomly selected real songs, verified artist/album metadata and actual covers. It is self-contained and simulates playback without audio or an account connection. The fictional test preview remains available for repeatable automated checks.\n- Gallery recordings: Dreams — Fleetwood Mac; Three Little Birds — Bob Marley & The Wailers; On Melancholy Hill — Gorillaz; Clocks — Coldplay; Feather (feat. Cise Starr & Akin) — Nujabes; No Ordinary Love — Sade. Artwork/catalog credits are included.\n\n## Updating\n\nExisting compatible installations: **Settings → Updates → Check now**, then choose Update. New users should install the full ZIP with Spotify desktop and compatible Spicetify. Normal updates preserve settings and local listening history. Installer scripts are unchanged.\n\nValidation uses simulated Spotify services and Windows bridges in Chromium. A signed-in Windows Spotify client and physical touchscreen were unavailable. See VALIDATION.md for exact checks.\n"
+  "notes": "# LiquidTouch v1.9.6 — Quality of life\n\n3 November 2026 · build `2026-11-03-qol`\n\n## Lyrics follow the current song\n\n- Every song begins with the lyrics panel hidden. With **Automatically show\n  found lyrics** enabled, the panel appears only after actual lyrics load for\n  the current song. Failed and empty lookups leave the centered player visible.\n- The player's **Lyrics** button can still open the panel when no lyrics were\n  found, so the status, Reload and source options remain accessible.\n- Manual show/hide applies to the current song. The next song starts hidden\n  again and checks its own lyrics. A late response from a previous song cannot\n  replace the current lyrics or open the panel.\n- Automatic lookup can be disabled in Settings without disabling manual lookup.\n\n## Consistent percentage controls\n\n- Every numeric Settings slider and its plus/minus buttons now use percentages\n  in **5% increments**, including opacity, text and control sizes, animation\n  speeds, keyboard/artwork size, blur, corner rounding, card tap timing and\n  gesture distances. Touch control size is adjustable as a percentage too.\n- A value of 100% means the standard amount for that control. Blur and opacity\n  can reach 0%; shorter timing/distance percentages shorten the relevant wait\n  or swipe. Existing values migrate to the nearest supported step.\n- Saved song limits remain actual counts, clock formats remain 12/24-hour\n  choices, and per-recording lyric timing remains a measured time correction.\n  These are not percentage sliders. Playback seek/volume retain their playback\n  precision.\n\n## Home stays current\n\n- A **Recently played** shelf follows actual playback contexts, albums and\n  artists as their metadata becomes available, including when playback changes\n  while another LiquidTouch page or the player is open.\n- Entries move to the front, are deduplicated and persist across normal updates\n  and restarts. The shelf retains up to 24 entities. Missing context metadata is\n  resolved from Spotify when that session permits it; no invented names or\n  artwork are inserted.\n- Home refreshes without changing the route or scroll position, and defers\n  rebuilds while a finger is down. Native Home snapshots replace stale native\n  recommendations instead of accumulating their old ordering indefinitely.\n\n## Less work in Low-RAM mode\n\n- Unchanged playback events now update progress without repainting track\n  metadata and playback controls. This optimization also benefits normal mode.\n- Low-RAM mode reduces background polling and DOM scans, skips animations and\n  adjacent-artwork preloading, releases hidden page fragments, removes blurred\n  image backgrounds and limits caches and artwork-fetch concurrency.\n- It uses smaller player artwork when Spotify supplies an appropriate image,\n  avoids a second image decode for color sampling, removes expensive blur and\n  shadow effects, and lets offscreen rows/cards skip rendering work.\n- The lyric clock runs only for an open player with visible timed lyrics and\n  slows down in Low-RAM mode. Hidden windows defer background polling.\n- Settings, local history, song limits and timing corrections remain saved.\n  The mode changes LiquidTouch's workload; actual total Spotify RAM and frame\n  rate depend on the client, library and PC, and have not been measured here.\n\n## Artwork and gestures\n\n- Removed the picture/settings shortcut over the Now Playing artwork. Artwork\n  sizing is in **Settings → Now Playing**. Artwork holds still open track options.\n- Left-edge swipes go Back throughout browsing, sheets and the player. Horizontal\n  browsing swipes change tabs. Horizontal swipes across player art, track\n  information, controls and lyrics skip tracks; the left edge retains Back.\n- Downward swipes collapse the player or dismiss the top sheet when the\n  relevant scroll container is at the top. Scrolled content continues scrolling.\n- Default track-swipe distances match between the mini player and expanded\n  player. Numeric thresholds are percentages, using the same 5% steps.\n- Fields, sliders, the keyboard and caption buttons keep their normal input.\n  Small, reversed, cancelled and differently angled gestures do not commit a\n  different action. Existing search recovery, held Backspace and artwork drag\n  prevention remain included.\n\n## Update and validation\n\nOn a compatible v1.9.0+ loader, open **Settings → Updates → Check now** and\nchoose Update. Both original and revised v1.9.5 installations can update to\nv1.9.6 normally. A fresh full installer is also included in the release package.\n\nThe complete built extension passed 23 DOM-host regression suites, including a\nreal v1.9.5 → v1.9.6 runtime update with settings/history preservation. The 12\nsearch/keyboard/hold controller suites and 7 installer tests also passed.\nSyntax, update/feed integrity, installer checksum and Git-bundle cloning are\nchecked as part of packaging. Chromium installation was unavailable; simulated\nDOM tests do not verify visual layout, hardware touch behavior or real Spotify\nperformance. See `VALIDATION.md` for the reproducible commands and limits.\n"
 }
